// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: protocols, conformance, implement, soundness
// @expected-error: E405
// @skip: T0812 is open — an implement block missing a required method
//        type-checks today and panics at the call. The spec states the
//        expectation and is the acceptance control for that task; the skip
//        comes off in the commit that adds the check, not by editing what
//        the spec expects.
//
// Pin (T0812) — an `implement P for T` block must provide every method P
// requires.
//
// The compiler checks that an implementation EXISTS, not that it is
// COMPLETE, and the two are checked by the same rule. Measured with a
// control in the same batch:
//
//     implement P for R { fn m(&self) -> Int { 7 } }   runs, 7
//     no implement at all, used at a bound <T: P>      E405        caught
//     implement P for R { }                            CLEAN -> panic
//
// So writing an EMPTY implementation silences the very diagnostic that
// catches the same mistake when the implementation is absent. A bound
// `T: P` then promises a method the type does not have, and the program
// type-checks its way to a guaranteed runtime panic.
//
// A protocol method WITH a body is a default and stays optional — 124 of
// those exist in core/, and a completeness check that ignores the
// distinction lights up every Iterator implementation in the library. This
// spec pins both halves: `polite` has a body and must NOT be demanded,
// `hello` has none and must be.
//
// Measured motivation, core/ on 2026-08-18: twelve implementations are
// incomplete. Four are `Debug` providing `fmt` where
// core/base/protocols.vr:303 requires `fmt_debug`, so debug-formatting
// those types panics rather than failing to compile.

type Greet is protocol {
    fn hello(&self) -> Text;
    fn polite(&self) -> Text { self.hello() }
};

type Silent is { v: Int };

implement Greet for Silent { }

fn main() {
    let s = Silent { v: 1 };
    print(s.polite());
}
